  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nThe northern portion of the mid level ridge breaks down as the center\nof the strong mid level high retrogrades west across the Lower MS \nValley into the Southern Plains overnight into Friday. This will \nallow the surface front to shift south into the Ohio Valley. Modest \n8H jet of 20-25 kts along with the front will result in stripes of \nconvection overnight. Forecast soundings suggest the potential for \nbackbuilding or training storms. With PWAT values around 2 inches \nthe signal exists for heavy rain and potential flooding. Feel\nmoderately confident regarding the heavy rain threat. Low confidence \nexists in the exact placement of these bands with the most favored\nregion from the southern I-70 corridor to the Ohio River. Expect this\nconvection to develop late tonight and end Friday morning as the\nfront slips south of the area. Have issued a flood watch for this \npotential beginning at midnight tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nA brief respite in rain will exist later Friday into early Saturday \nas the front stalls south of the area. Somewhat drier air will work \ninto the north but dewpoints in the mid 60s lingers in the south. \nIn the wake of the front increased cloud cover will result in lower \ntemperatures on Friday with highs from the upper 70s north to the mid\n80s south. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms return to the forecast later Saturday as \nmoisture increases. This threat continues Sunday with another \nsagging south into the area. An unsettled weather pattern with\nshowers and storms remaining in the forecast thru midweek. \nTemperatures will gradually trend cooler each day as we head into mid\nweek with readings forecast closer to normal.",
